---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { doctor, site } from '../data/site.js';
---

<Page
  title="Telemedicine consent"
  description="What you are agreeing to when you book an online consultation, what it can and cannot do, and how prescribing works."
  slug="telemedicine-consent"
  updated="2026-10-17"
  intro="Read this before your online consultation. It sets out what a video consultation can do, what it cannot, and what you are consenting to."
  showByline={true}
>
  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>Before publishing</h3>
    <p>
      This draft follows the Telemedicine Practice Guidelines issued under India's medical council
      regulations. It should be checked against the current text of those guidelines, and reviewed
      by a lawyer advising medical practices, before the site goes live.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who you are consulting</h2>
  <p>
    {doctor.name}, {doctor.qualifications} (Dermatology, Venereology and Leprology), registered
    with the {doctor.registration}. Registration can be verified on the Delhi Medical Council
    register.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your identity will also be confirmed at the start of the consultation. This is a requirement
    of the guidelines, not a formality.
  </p>

  <h2>What you are consenting to</h2>
  <p>By proceeding with an online consultation you agree that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You are consulting a registered medical practitioner over video</li>
    <li>You consent to the consultation taking place remotely</li>
    <li>You will give accurate information about your symptoms, history and current medicines</li>
    <li>A clinical record of the consultation will be created and retained</li>
    <li>Photographs you send become part of that clinical record</li>
    <li>You may withdraw consent and end the consultation at any point</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a video consultation can and cannot do</h2>
  <p>
    A remote consultation is a weaker diagnostic setting than a clinic. A full history can be
    taken, and many skin and hair conditions are clearly visible in good photographs. But some
    things cannot be done over video, and saying so plainly is part of doing this properly.
  </p>

  <h3>Cannot be done remotely</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Dermoscopy or trichoscopy — magnified examination of skin and scalp</li>
    <li>A KOH mount to confirm or exclude fungal infection</li>
    <li>A biopsy, swab or patch test</li>
    <li>Any procedure, injection or minor surgery</li>
    <li>Assessment of a mole or growth that is changing, which needs examination</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If your condition requires any of these, you will be told at the start of the consultation
    and advised where to be seen. In that situation the consultation fee is not taken.
  </p>

  <h2>Prescribing</h2>
  <p>
    The Telemedicine Practice Guidelines place medicines into categories and restrict what may be
    prescribed in a first remote consultation. Some medicines cannot be prescribed remotely at
    all, and some require an in-person examination first.
  </p>
  <p>
    This affects dermatology directly. Isotretinoin for acne, and anti-androgen medicines used for
    hair loss, both carry significant considerations and restrictions. Where such a medicine is
    being considered, the risks and alternatives will be discussed with you before anything is
    prescribed, and you will receive a written record of that discussion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Any prescription issued carries the prescriber's name and registration number.
  </p>

  <h2>Photographs</h2>
  <p>
    Clear photographs materially improve what can be assessed remotely. Take them in natural
    daylight, without a filter, with one wide view and one close-up of each affected area.
  </p>
  <p>
    Photographs are stored as part of your confidential clinical record. They are never used
    publicly — on this website, on social media or in teaching — without separate written consent
    that you can withdraw.
  </p>

  <h2>Confidentiality and its limits</h2>
  <p>
    Your consultation and records are confidential. Information is not shared with family
    members or anyone else without your consent, except where disclosure is required by law.
  </p>
  <p>
    Video consultations take place over a standard internet connection. Please take the call
    somewhere private.
  </p>

  <h2>If the technology fails</h2>
  <p>
    If the connection fails before the consultation can be completed, it will be rescheduled or
    the fee refunded. You are welcome to request an audio consultation if video is not working,
    though for dermatology this limits what can be assessed and you will be told if it is not
    adequate.
  </p>

  <h2>This is not an emergency service</h2>
  <p>
    Do not book an online consultation for a rapidly spreading rash, blistering, a severe
    allergic reaction, or any skin problem with fever or feeling unwell. Seek care in person
    immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If anything here is unclear, ask before your consultation rather than during it:
    <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
